Chemotherapy/Radiotherapy-Induced Nausea, Vomiting (CINV), and Cachexia
Haematology & Oncology
Severe post-treatment nausea/vomiting, Anticipatory nausea, Profound anorexia/weight loss, Visible skeletal muscle wasting

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-011
Reason for Visit: Uncontrolled emesis leading to acute dehydration and orthostatic instability requiring immediate parenteral intervention.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and caregiver
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage established a 12-hour history of relentless, profuse vomiting immediately following chemotherapy, leaving the patient unable to retain oral fluids or oral anti-emetics. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to evaluate severe volume depletion and administer intramuscular anti-emetics, preventing life-threatening hypovolaemic shock or acute kidney injury prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I cannot stop dry heaving, every time I sip water it comes straight back up, and I have absolutely no energy left."
History of Present Illness: The patient details a rapid progression of severe nausea commencing 4 hours post-chemotherapy yesterday. This escalated to profuse, recurrent vomiting overnight, resulting in physical exhaustion and total inability to tolerate oral intake. They report profound anorexia, describing a complete loss of interest in food and altered taste sensation (dysgeusia). Over the past two months of treatment, they have experienced severe generalized fatigue and an unintentional 8 percent weight loss.
Functional Status: Bedbound, completely dependent on the caregiver for immediate hygiene needs due to severe weakness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver distress; extreme fall risk secondary to hypovolaemia upon standing.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 54 Kg
General: Cachectic, frail adult appearing visibly exhausted and chronically ill. Dry, cracked lips.
Physical Exam:
Gastrointestinal: Abdomen scaphoid, soft, non-tender. Bowel sounds present but hypoactive.
Skin/Musculoskeletal: Visible severe wasting of skeletal muscle mass at the temples and shoulder girdles. Poor skin turgor with delayed capillary refill (3 seconds).
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm. Weak peripheral pulses.
Environmental Observations: Multiple emesis basins and soiled towels located bedside.
Medication Reconciliation: Oral ondansetron prescribed but vomited immediately upon ingestion twice tonight.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chemotherapy/Radiotherapy-Induced Nausea, Vomiting, and Cachexia - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: absence of colicky abdominal pain, lack of abdominal distension, and direct temporal link to chemotherapy administration.

Acute Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: absence of fever, absence of diarrhoea, and immediate onset correlating strictly with the antineoplastic therapy schedule.

Peptic Ulcer Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: lack of localized epigastric pain, absence of haematemesis or melena, and presence of profound systemic cachexia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe, refractory post-treatment vomiting and profound cachexia resulting in acute clinical dehydration and tachycardia. The inability to tolerate oral anti-emetics creates a compounding cycle of volume depletion. Delaying physical assessment and intervention until normal clinic hours would expose this frail, immunocompromised patient to an unacceptable risk of severe acute kidney injury, electrolyte derangement (hypokalaemia), and cardiovascular collapse. The presentation strictly meets criteria for an urgent after-hours home visit to deliver immediate parenteral symptom control and evaluate for emergency hospitalization.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular metoclopramide 10mg stat. Arranged urgent outpatient laboratory tests for urea, electrolytes, and creatinine for tomorrow morning.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Take one teaspoon of oral rehydration solution every 15 minutes instead of drinking from a cup. By metering your fluid intake strictly, you avoid stretching the stomach wall, which directly prevents the reflex trigger to vomit.

Have your caregiver prepare all food in a separate room and keep the bedroom well-ventilated. By removing all food odors from your immediate sensory environment, you eliminate the olfactory triggers that cause anticipatory nausea.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to community nursing for potential in-home subcutaneous fluid administration.
DME/Safety (Optional): Bedside commode.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 12 hours via telehealth to confirm cessation of vomiting and adequate urine output.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department or call 000 if you cannot keep down a teaspoon of fluid after one hour, if you stop passing urine for 8 hours, or if you become confused.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis is dependent on the known side effects of the recent chemotherapy; severe dehydration requires ongoing monitoring.
Clinical Course: The injection will bypass your stomach to halt the vomiting reflex centrally. Over the next 24 hours, the priority is slowly restoring fluid balance.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk to the bathroom unassisted. If you feel dizzy while lying flat or fail to urinate by morning, proceed to the Emergency Department.

PATIENT EDUCATION

